# WHAT IS THIS FILE?
# .gitignore tells git which files/folders to NEVER track or offer to
# commit — things like generated logs, local secrets, or build output that
# shouldn't be part of the project's saved history. Git checks this file
# automatically every time you run commands like `git status` or `git add`;
# you never run .gitignore directly. Each non-comment line is a pattern: a
# leading `/` anchors it to the project root, a trailing `/` means "only
# match a directory," a leading `!` means "don't ignore this" (re-including
# something a broader pattern above already excluded), and `*` is a
# wildcard matching any run of characters.

# `#` starts a comment, ignored by git — this points to GitHub's own help
# page on ignoring files, plus a note about a DIFFERENT ignore mechanism.
# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files for more about ignoring files.
#
# This comment line explains that machine-specific junk (like editor swap
# files or OS files such as macOS's .DS_Store) does NOT belong in this
# project's own .gitignore — because this file is shared and committed for
# everyone on the team, but "my text editor leaves .swp files" is specific
# to one person's machine. Git has a separate, per-machine ignore file for
# exactly that purpose, referenced below.
# Temporary files generated by your text editor or operating system
# belong in git's global ignore instead:
# `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore` or `~/.config/git/ignore`

# Blank line — visual separation.

# Ignore bundler config.
# `/.bundle` excludes the local Bundler configuration folder — as in
# .dockerignore, this can hold machine-specific settings that shouldn't be
# shared/committed, since every developer's Bundler setup may differ.
/.bundle

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore all environment files.
# `/.env*` excludes any file starting with ".env" — these typically hold
# local secrets (API keys, passwords) that must never be committed to
# source control, where they'd become permanently visible in git history.
/.env*

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore all logfiles and tempfiles.
# `/log/*` excludes everything currently inside the log/ directory — log
# output is generated by running the app locally, not something to track.
/log/*
# `/tmp/*` excludes everything inside tmp/ — transient runtime files
# (caches, locks, etc.) regenerated automatically as needed.
/tmp/*
# `!/log/.keep` re-includes the log/.keep placeholder file specifically, so
# git still tracks (and thus preserves) the otherwise-empty log/ directory
# — git doesn't track empty folders at all by default, only files, so a
# folder with nothing "kept" in it would simply vanish from a fresh clone.
!/log/.keep
# `!/tmp/.keep` does the same for tmp/ — keeps the empty directory present
# in every checkout of this repository.
!/tmp/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore pidfiles, but keep the directory.
# `/tmp/pids/*` excludes the contents of tmp/pids/ — pid files record a
# running process's ID and are pure runtime state, never something to
# version.
/tmp/pids/*
# `!/tmp/pids/` re-includes the tmp/pids/ directory itself (note: unlike
# the .dockerignore version of this file, this line re-includes the FOLDER
# entry, not just a .keep file inside it — belt-and-suspenders alongside
# the .keep re-inclusion on the next line).
!/tmp/pids/
# `!/tmp/pids/.keep` re-includes the placeholder file inside tmp/pids/, so
# the directory is guaranteed to survive as empty-but-present in a fresh
# checkout even though its real runtime contents stay ignored.
!/tmp/pids/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore storage (uploaded files in development and any SQLite databases).
# `/storage/*` excludes everything under storage/ — Active Storage uploads
# and/or SQLite database files live here, and neither belongs in git (they
# contain runtime/user data, not source code, and can also grow large).
/storage/*
# `!/storage/.keep` re-includes the placeholder file so the empty storage/
# directory itself is still tracked/present after a fresh checkout.
!/storage/.keep
# `/tmp/storage/*` excludes the contents of a second, tmp-scoped storage
# location (used for some test/dev-only file storage).
/tmp/storage/*
# `!/tmp/storage/` re-includes that folder itself, same reasoning as the
# tmp/pids/ case above.
!/tmp/storage/
# `!/tmp/storage/.keep` re-includes its placeholder file too, guaranteeing
# the empty directory persists across checkouts.
!/tmp/storage/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# `/public/assets` excludes precompiled production asset output (bundled/
# fingerprinted CSS, JS, images) — this is BUILD OUTPUT regenerated from
# source files already in the repo, so committing it would just be
# duplicated, quickly-stale binary/text noise. Note this line has no
# preceding "# Ignore ..." comment of its own in the original file — it's
# grouped visually with the surrounding blank lines instead.
/public/assets

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore key files for decrypting credentials and more.
# `/config/*.key` excludes any file directly inside config/ ending in
# ".key" (e.g. config/master.key) — these are the secret keys used to
# decrypt config/credentials.yml.enc and must never be committed, since
# anyone who can read the git history could then decrypt all app secrets.
/config/*.key

# Blank separator line.
# Second blank separator line (two blank lines in a row here in the
# original file — has no special meaning, git treats any number of
# consecutive blank lines the same as one).

# `/app/assets/builds/*` excludes compiled frontend asset output living
# under app/assets/builds/ (e.g. output from a JS/CSS build tool) — like
# public/assets above, this is generated from source and shouldn't be
# committed. Also has no preceding "# Ignore ..." comment line of its own.
/app/assets/builds/*
# `!/app/assets/builds/.keep` re-includes the placeholder file so the
# empty builds/ directory persists across checkouts, ready for a build
# step to populate later.
!/app/assets/builds/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore crawl error screenshots (app/services/companies/base_parser.rb) and
# recon tool output (app/services/recon/recon_service.rb) — both are
# regenerated debug artifacts from running the app, not source code.
# (This comment block was already here — it explains that this app has web
# crawling/scraping and recon features, and both write out debug files as
# they run; those debug files are useless to keep in version control since
# they're recreated fresh on each run and can pile up / contain scraped
# third-party content.)
# `/logs/*` excludes the contents of a `logs/` folder (note: plural, and
# DIFFERENT from the singular `/log/` ignored above near the top of this
# file — Rails' own convention is the singular `log/`, so this looks like
# a second, app-specific debug-output folder rather than a typo of the
# first one; see the flagged note about this at the end of this pass).
/logs/*
# `/recon/*` excludes the contents of a `recon/` folder — output from the
# "recon" feature referenced in the comment above.
/recon/*
